<h1>Introduction to Routing in ASP.NET Core MVC</h1>

<p>
    Routing is the process of matching an incoming URL
    to a controller and action method.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Why Routing is Needed?</h2>

<p>
    When a user sends a request to the application,
    ASP.NET Core must decide:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Which controller should handle the request?</li>
    <li>Which action method should execute?</li>
    <li>What parameter values should be passed?</li>
</ul>

<p>
    Routing solves this problem by mapping URLs to endpoints.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Simple Example</h2>

<pre>
<code>
https://localhost:5001/Product/Details/5
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Routing extracts:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Product → Controller</li>
    <li>Details → Action Method</li>
    <li>5 → Route Parameter</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>How Routing Works Internally</h2>

<pre>
<code>
Browser Request
        ↓
Middleware Pipeline
        ↓
Routing Middleware
        ↓
Controller
        ↓
Action Method
        ↓
Response Returned
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Routing Middleware</h2>

<p>
    Routing works using the routing middleware.
</p>

<p>
    In ASP.NET Core, routing middleware is enabled using:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
app.UseRouting();
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    This middleware analyzes the incoming URL
    and finds the matching endpoint.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>What is an Endpoint?</h2>

<p>
    An endpoint is the final destination of a request.
</p>

<p>
    In MVC applications:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
Controller Action = Endpoint
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Example:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
/Product/Details
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    This URL points to a specific controller action.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Types of Routing</h2>

<h3>1. Conventional Routing</h3>

<p>
    Routes are defined centrally inside Program.cs.
</p>

<pre>
<code>
app.MapControllerRoute(
    name: "default",
    pattern: "{controller=Home}/{action=Index}/{id?}");
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h3>2. Attribute Routing</h3>

<p>
    Routes are defined directly on controllers or actions.
</p>

<pre>
<code>
[Route("products/all")]
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Conventional Routing vs Attribute Routing</h2>

<table border="1" cellpadding="10">

    <tr>
        <th>Conventional Routing</th>
        <th>Attribute Routing</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>Defined in Program.cs</td>
        <td>Defined on Controllers/Actions</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>Centralized Routing</td>
        <td>Distributed Routing</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>Best for MVC Applications</td>
        <td>Best for Web APIs</td>
    </tr>

</table>

<hr />

<h2>Important Interview Points</h2>

<ul>
    <li>
        Routing is the process of mapping incoming requests
        to controller actions or endpoints.
    </li>

    <li>
        Routing happens after middleware execution begins
        and before controller action execution.
    </li>

    <li>
        Routing middleware analyzes the incoming URL
        and finds the matching endpoint.
    </li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Final Conclusion</h2>

<p>
    Routing is one of the core concepts in ASP.NET Core MVC.
    It helps the framework determine which controller and action
    should handle an incoming request.
</p>